## Type-Safe Wrappers

### The Problem

```xiom
fn create_user(name: Str, age: Int, salary: Int) -> User {
  // age and salary are both Int — easy to swap accidentally!
}

create_user("Alice", 30, 50000);  // Did we pass age or salary second?
```

### The Solution: Newtype

```xiom
pub type UserId = Int;
pub type Age = Int;
pub type Salary = Int;

fn create_user(name: Str, age: Age, salary: Salary) -> User {
  // Compiler prevents type mixups!
}

let uid: UserId = 42.into();
let age: Age = 30.into();
let salary: Salary = 50000.into();

create_user("Alice", age, salary);   // Correct
// create_user("Alice", salary, age);  // COMPILE ERROR: expected Age, got Salary
```

### Newtype + Validation

```xiom
pub type Email = Str
  invariant: this.contains("@");

pub type PhoneNumber = Str
  invariant: this.len() >= 10;

fn send_email(to: Email, body: Str) { /* ... */ }
fn send_sms(to: PhoneNumber, body: Str) { /* ... */ }

// send_email(phone, body);  // Compile error — can't pass PhoneNumber as Email
```

### Newtype + Interfaces

```xiom
pub type Meters = Float64;
impl From[Float64] for Meters { fn from(v: Float64) -> Meters { return v; } }

pub type Kilometers = Float64;
impl From[Float64] for Kilometers { fn from(v: Float64) -> Kilometers { return v; } }

fn calculate_speed(distance: Meters, time: Float64) -> Float64 {
  return distance / time;
}

let d: Meters = 100.0.into();
let k: Kilometers = 1.0.into();
// calculate_speed(k, 10.0);  // COMPILE ERROR — Kilometers is not Meters!
```

### Zero Cost

Newtype wrappers have ZERO runtime overhead — they're compiled away to the underlying type. The safety is purely a compile-time construct.

### Real-World Examples

| Newtype | Wraps | Prevents |
|---------|-------|----------|
| UserId | Int | Mixing user IDs with counts |
| Password | Str | Accidental logging of secrets |
| Temperature | Float64 | Celsius vs Fahrenheit confusion |
| FileSize | Int | Bytes vs kilobytes confusion |

